                <pb facs="1015" n="919" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg919"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.162/9: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Salvador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Schuyler</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >San Salvador</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-01-14T13:00:00-06:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 14,
                                1925—1 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 10:20
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>2. Referring to most-favored-nation exchange of notes. The Minister for Foreign
                    Affairs suggests informally as <hi rendition="#italic">quid pro quo</hi> that
                    United States shall grant Salvadorean and preferably all Central American sugar
                    same rates as now given Cuban sugar. I have explained fully peculiar reciprocal
                    nature of article 8 of our commercial convention with Cuba but he requests that
                    I forward his suggestion to you.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schuyler</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
